<section>
    <section>
        <h2>Motivation</h2>
        <p>When designing ontologies, assessing whether they capture required data without introducing inconsistencies is critical.</p>
        <p>Producing synthetic data that exercises the ontology is time-consuming—especially across multiple iterations of complex or evolving data models.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>Research gap</h3>
        <p>Existing LLM-based ontology tools focus primarily on <strong>ontology design support</strong>.</p>
        <p>OWL-SDA takes a <strong>data-driven evaluation approach</strong>: generate and validate synthetic data rather than rely on LLMs to design the ontology.</p>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <section>
        <h2>OWL-SDA</h2>
        <p>An open-source multi-agent tool that uses AI agents to efficiently generate and validate synthetic data expressed in OWL.</p>
        <div class="mermaid">
            flowchart LR
                ONTOLOGY[("OWL Ontology")]
                GENERATOR("🤖 Generator Agent")
                VALIDATOR("✅ Validator Agent")
                DATA[("Synthetic Data")]

                ONTOLOGY --> GENERATOR
                GENERATOR --> DATA
                DATA --> VALIDATOR
                VALIDATOR -->|"Feedback"| GENERATOR
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Evaluation</h2>
    <p>Demonstrated on two domain-specific ontologies:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Industrial emissions reporting</strong> (RIE-IEPR, Flemish Government)</li>
        <li><strong>Healthcare ontology</strong> (UZ Brussel)</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>OWL-SDA successfully surfaced inconsistencies and missing relations in both ontologies across multiple generation iterations.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Conclusion &amp; Future Work</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>OWL-SDA automates synthetic data generation for ontology evaluation</li>
        <li>Multi-agent validation loop reduces manual effort</li>
        <li>Future: support for SHACL constraints, larger ontologies, benchmarking</li>
    </ul>
    <p><small>Source code available on GitHub · SEMANTICS 2026</small></p>
</section>
